WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:05.650
Since it premiered on an obscure Canadian
cable channel in 2001, Trailer Park Boys has

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:10.970
gone from an underground cult favorite to
an international sensation, as audiences around

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:15.740
the world warmed to the foul-mouthed antics
of Ricky, Julian, and Bubbles.

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:19.550
But how much do you really know about the
people behind the show?

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:23.150
Here's a look at the untold truth of Trailer
Park Boys.

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:25.180
Almost nobody was a professional

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:29.600
Instead of professional actors, many of the
original cast were friends of creator Mike

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:30.630
Clattenburg.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:35.339
Robb Wells and John Paul Tremblay, who play
Ricky and Julian, met Clattenburg in high

00:00:35.339 --> 00:00:40.050
school and went on to run a pub and pizzeria
on Prince Edward Island before leaving to

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:41.329
work on the show.

00:00:41.329 --> 00:00:45.859
Another of Clattenburg's buddies was Mike
Smith, who was so funny when he visited the

00:00:45.859 --> 00:00:50.719
set to record some audio tracks that they
added him to the cast as Bubbles.

00:00:50.719 --> 00:00:55.769
John Dunsworth, who plays Jim Lahey, had the
most experience out of the original cast,

00:00:55.769 --> 00:01:01.549
having worked steadily as an actor since the
1960s, while J-Roc actor Jonathan Torrens

00:01:01.549 --> 00:01:07.160
also had some experience as the host of a
teen-oriented talk show called Jonovision.

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:08.160
Close call

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:13.870
After an early career as a local TV producer
in Halifax, Nova Scotia, Clattenburg shot

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:17.570
a feature-length Trailer Park Boys pilot on
his own dime.

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:21.400
In 2000, he flew to Toronto to pitch the show
to TV executives.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:26.710
Unfortunately, a mockumentary about a group
of foul-mouthed petty criminals wasn't an

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:27.990
easy sell.

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:30.890
Every network gave him a firm no.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:34.740
Clattenburg had actually given up and was
preparing to return to Nova Scotia when he

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:36.950
decided to give it one more shot.

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:41.810
He and producer Barrie Dunn cold-called Showcase,
a relatively new cable channel that was still

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:43.630
trying to find its niche.

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:48.810
To their shock, the vice president of programming
picked up the phone and they were in her office

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:51.360
pitching the show in 25 minutes.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:56.780
Showcase loved the pilot, and the Trailer
Park Boys series was given the green light.

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:57.780
Slow burn

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:03.530
Trailer Park Boys debuted on Showcase in 2001,
but it didn't exactly set the world alight

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:04.930
in its first season.

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:10.060
Michael Jackson, who played Trevor, says that
one early fan organized a party to celebrate

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:13.310
the show and the whole cast decided to go.

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:18.050
Unfortunately, no one else showed up and the
cast spent the night partying with their one

00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:19.050
fan.

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:23.709
Fortunately, Showcase was a struggling young
channel in need of content and played the

00:02:23.709 --> 00:02:27.780
episodes on repeat until they picked up a
cult following across North America.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:33.239
When the cast visited Los Angeles in 2004,
they were shocked to find everyone knew who

00:02:33.239 --> 00:02:37.889
they were despite the fact that she show had
never aired in the U.S.

00:02:37.889 --> 00:02:40.040
Sounds like they really turned up the heat.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:40.660
“Look at me!”

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:41.160
“What?”

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:44.000
“Turnips aren’t involved in the expression!”

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:47.900
“Well, that’s just your f---in' opinion,
isn’t it?”

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:48.939
Smoke free

00:02:48.939 --> 00:02:53.680
The cast of Trailer Park Boys are known for
their love of booze and weed, even signing

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:56.620
a deal to have their own brand of marijuana.

00:02:56.620 --> 00:03:01.060
One thing they don't do, though, is actually
smoke or drink on camera.

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:05.900
For smoking scenes, Robb Wells has said that
they're often actually toking on rosemary.

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:11.109
And the pot plants grown by Ricky are actually
perfectly legal industrial hemp plants, which

00:03:11.109 --> 00:03:13.780
do not contain any THC.

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:18.590
That said, you can spot real cannabis at least
once in the series, with Wells explaining

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:21.810
that "...there was one time when we were doing
bottle hits.

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:25.650
They were trying to get this fake stuff to
burn like hash and they couldn't find anything

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:29.400
that worked, so I think they used real hash
for that one."

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:30.400
“Bubbles, hit this s---.

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:31.800
All the way from California, baby.”

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:34.460
“Oh my God, one of Snoop Dogg’s real joints!”

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:37.370
One Last Shot

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:42.120
A major inspiration for the series was a short
film Clattenburg made in 1998.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:47.340
The short, called One Last Shot, featured
John Paul Tremblay and Robb Wells as early

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:49.260
versions of Julian and Ricky.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:54.579
The film was entirely improvised on set and
John Dunsworth apparently stole the show in

00:03:54.579 --> 00:03:59.569
his first appearance as Mr. Lahey, to the
point that he nabbed a best performance award

00:03:59.569 --> 00:04:01.500
at the Atlantic Film Festival.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:05.889
Sadly, One Last Shot has never been commercially
released.

00:04:05.889 --> 00:04:07.090
Old lady glasses

00:04:07.090 --> 00:04:12.019
Mike Smith's Bubbles quickly became the show's
breakout character, with his friendly attitude

00:04:12.019 --> 00:04:17.570
complemented by a pair of adorably thick glasses,
which are actually older than the character.

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:22.620
In fact, his girlfriend found them at an estate
sale in Texas for 50 cents among the effects

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:25.070
of a recently deceased old lady.

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:29.280
Smith loved the glasses and would put them
on to amuse friends, which was the origin

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:31.160
of the "Bubbles" character.

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:35.500
The glasses originally gave him a pounding
headache, but over the years his eyes have

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:38.200
adjusted to the ridiculous prescription.

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:39.730
Angry actors

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:44.780
After six seasons, the actors who played Cory
and Trevor left the show, complaining of low

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:47.220
pay and poor working conditions.

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:52.660
Cory Bowles eventually returned as Cory, but
Michael Jackson refused to make another appearance

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:58.840
as Trevor, posting that he felt ripped off,
and, quote, "disrespected as an actor and

00:04:58.840 --> 00:04:59.900
as a person."

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:04.780
According to Jackson, he worked for the minimum
actor's equity pay for the first three seasons,

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:09.120
then received only tiny raises despite the
show's growing popularity.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:13.900
By Season 5, friends who appeared in commercials
were still making more than he was.

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:18.760
To make matters worse, he said that the producers
repeatedly lied to him and the other actors

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:23.790
about other actors' pay, refused to hire a
proper film crew, and provided unsanitary

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:25.710
bathrooms and trailers.

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:30.280
John Paul Tremblay later hit back at Jackson
on a Trailer Park Boys forum, claiming that

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:34.610
any difference in pay was due to the fact
that the primary cast members also worked

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:39.610
as full-time writers on the show for what
worked out to "pennies on the hour."

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:41.390
Abuse accusations

00:05:41.390 --> 00:05:44.990
Fans love Bubbles for his gentle, childlike
nature.

00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:50.580
So they were shocked in 2016 when Mike Smith
was arrested in a Los Angeles hotel and charged

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:54.120
with domestic battery against a woman named
Georgia Ling.

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:57.640
But the charges were quickly dropped, with
Ling releasing a statement saying,

00:05:57.640 --> 00:06:02.830
"...at no point did I feel I was in danger,
otherwise I would've called the police myself,

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:04.220
which I did not.

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:09.210
The police were called by others not present
in the room who mistakenly perceived the argument

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:11.580
to be something other than what it was."

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:16.240
Actress Lucy DeCoutere, who plays "Lucy,"
further muddied the waters when she announced

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:21.670
she was leaving the show with a tweet that
read "If I find out that somebody is abusive,

00:06:21.670 --> 00:06:23.390
I cut them out of my life.

00:06:23.390 --> 00:06:24.390
It's very easy."

00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:29.670
However, the show's publicist contradicted
this statement, insisting that DeCoutere had

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:32.430
informed producers she was quitting weeks
earlier.

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:37.490
DeCoutere later tweeted "the TPB cast and
crew are still my friends and there are no

00:06:37.490 --> 00:06:39.970
bad feelings between us."

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:40.970
Burger time

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:45.920
During their recent live tour, the boys earned
some extra cash by having fans come up on

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:48.760
stage to buy hamburgers for $10 a pop.

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:53.240
At some shows the burgers were cooked live
on a George Foreman grill, while at other

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:57.020
shows, they apparently were just McDonald's
cheeseburgers wrapped in paper.

00:06:57.020 --> 00:07:02.240
VICE later estimated that Julian raked in
at least $1,500 at a show in Oakland, with

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:06.610
drunk and stoned audience members queuing
up on stage to buy the burgers.

00:07:06.610 --> 00:07:11.020
In an interview to promote the U.K. leg of
the tour, John Paul Tremblay insisted that

00:07:11.020 --> 00:07:12.710
the stunt "wasn't a scam!

00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:14.190
Those people got their cheeseburgers!"

00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:16.250
It certainly could have been worse.

00:07:16.250 --> 00:07:20.110
[Burp]
“Ricky, there’s onion ring fragments on

00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:21.110
me!

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:22.310
Get them off!”

00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:24.330
It won't end anytime soon

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:31.020
After over 10 seasons, multiple movies, an
international live tour, and numerous minisodes

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:34.190
and specials, the boys could be forgiven for
wanting to take a break.

00:07:34.190 --> 00:07:35.190
But they show no signs of slowing down.

00:07:35.190 --> 00:07:36.390
As the boys told Metro News,

00:07:36.390 --> 00:07:41.590
"It's funny to watch the characters get older…
where 20 years from now you see Ricky, Julian

00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:43.680
and Bubbles who are in their late 60s…

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:48.330
We want to continue shooting until our fans
say 'Guys, it's time to shut it down.'"

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:49.330
Thanks for watching!

00:07:49.330 --> 00:07:52.270
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:52.270 --> 00:07:54.800
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

